    remediation: [
      { prompt: "Under § 858-353(A)(2), what must a broker do with offers?", choices: ["Unless specifically waived in writing by a party: receive all written offers and counteroffers; reduce offers or counteroffers to a written form upon request of ANY party; and present timely all written offers and counteroffers.", "Present only offers that meet the seller's stated minimum.", "Present offers until the property goes under contract.", "Present offers from represented buyers only."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "Read (b) again: upon request of ANY party — including one you are not serving. And nothing in the paragraph suspends the duty when the property goes under contract. The waiver is the party's, specific, and in writing; an oral 'don't bring me lowballs' is neither." },
      { prompt: "Under § 858-361, may an Oklahoma licensee use the word 'agent'?", choices: ["Yes — in a trade name and as a general reference for designating themselves as a licensee, even though § 858-360(B) means no agency relationship exists.", "No; the word is prohibited by § 858-360(B).", "Only where the licensee is serving a single party.", "Only with the written consent of the party served."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "The word survives as vocabulary while the legal relationship it names does not. A licensee who says 'I'm your agent' has not violated anything; a licensee who BELIEVES it and acts on common-law fiduciary instincts instead of the statute will breach the statute sooner or later." },
      { prompt: "Under § 858-358, what survives the termination of a brokerage relationship?", choices: ["Except as a written brokerage agreement provides, only two duties — to account for all monies and property relating to the transaction, and to keep confidential all confidential information received during the relationship.", "All seven duties, for one further year.", "Nothing; every duty ends on termination.", "Only the duty to account."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "A listing expires and six months later you are working the same house for a buyer. The former seller's motivating factors and willingness to accept less are still § 858-353(A)(4) items and § 858-358(2) keeps them in. Note the opening words: a written brokerage agreement may extend duties, so read it." },
      { prompt: "Under § 858-359(A), what does payment of compensation by a party establish?", choices: ["Nothing about the relationship — payment or promise of payment does not determine what relationship, if any, has been established between the broker and a party.", "That the paying party is the broker's client.", "That the broker is serving the paying party exclusively.", "A subagency between the paying party's broker and the recipient."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "And § 858-359(B) adds that receiving a fee based on a selling price or lease cost is not itself a breach of duty. Note also § 858-312(4): an associate may accept compensation only from the broker with whom the associate is associated." },
      { prompt: "Under § 858-363, in whose name may an associate enter a brokerage agreement?", choices: ["Only in the name of the broker — associates shall not enter into a brokerage agreement with a party in the associate's name.", "In the associate's own name, once licensed.", "In either name, at the associate's option.", "In the name of the party being served."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "The broker may authorise associates to provide brokerage services in the broker's name, which may include executing written agreements — so know the scope of your authorisation. § 858-312(5) adds that representing a broker other than the one you are associated with, without their express knowledge and consent, is a ground for discipline." }
    ]
