      { prompt: "Under § 858-312(17), what is a ground for suspension or revocation of an Oklahoma real estate licence?", choices: ["Conviction in a court of competent jurisdiction of having violated any provision of the federal fair housing laws, 42 U.S.C. § 3601 et seq.", "Any complaint filed with HUD alleging a fair housing violation.", "Advertising without disclosing licensee status.", "Failing to display an equal housing opportunity logo."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "And § 858-353(A)(6) — a duty to all parties, mandatory and unwaivable — requires compliance with all applicable statutes and rules, which includes the federal fair housing laws. So the tracks run in parallel and neither waits for the other." },
      { prompt: "A tenant with a disability asks to install a grab bar. What is that, and who ordinarily pays?", choices: ["A reasonable modification — a physical change, generally at the requesting person's expense; a reasonable accommodation is a change to a rule, policy or service, generally at the provider's expense.", "A reasonable accommodation, at the provider's expense.", "A capital improvement the landlord must fund.", "A lease violation unless the landlord consents in advance."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "Rule-change versus physical-change is the whole distinction and it is tested constantly. An assistance animal is not a pet: pet rules, pet rent and pet deposits do not apply, and the questions that may be asked about it are narrowly limited." },
      { prompt: "A licensee says 'you'd probably be more comfortable on the north side.' What is that?", choices: ["Steering — directing a prospect toward or away from an area on the basis of a protected characteristic, which is committed by omission as easily as by statement.", "Permissible, because no protected class was named.", "Blockbusting.", "Redlining."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "Steering rarely announces itself; it arrives as a helpful remark. The prevention is to answer with data the buyer can verify and to describe property, never people — and a buyer's OWN request to search on a protected characteristic does not create an exemption." },
      { prompt: "Which classes does ECOA protect that the Fair Housing Act does not?", choices: ["Marital status and age, provided the applicant can contract — along with income derived from a public assistance programme.", "Familial status and disability.", "Source of funds and occupation.", "Veteran status and citizenship."], correctChoice: 0, rationale: "ECOA governs credit rather than housing, and a rejected applicant is entitled to notice of the reasons. The ADA covers a third area again — places of public accommodation under Title III, which includes a real estate office itself." }
